Andre has some valid points and before you dismiss them I ask you to consider the following; 
Treatment programs have no legal obligation to implement completion dates or to provide patients with emotional supports while receiving treatment.
Why this presents a problem;
A person is now subjected to a lifetime of pharmaceutical dependency with no end in sight and still the person is no closer to addressing the issues that the compulsive coping behavior stemmed from.
Another issue is the health care providers and pharmacies that profit from the life long struggles of our most vulnerable in society. 
Often it's the truth that is the most unpopular idea expressed in the room and instead of sweeping another issue under the rug with yet another blanket approach that historically has never been successful, we start to make mental health, vulnerable sector and poverty the focus. Not as convenient as a pill or a liquid and there are no immediate financial gains but this fix would stick.

